INT. GRAND HOTEL MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - HALL OF PENDULUMS - EVENING

A cavernous gallery lined with grandfather clocks, their brass pendulums swinging out of step with one another like a crowd of mourners nodding at different tunes. The parquet gleams. Tall windows frame a bruised sunset.

Gathered at the center beneath a crystal chandelier: INSPECTOR AUGUSTINE FELL (50s, trench coat damp at the shoulders, fedora in hand), a methodical man with a notebook the size of a prayer book.

MRS. ODETTE LAMBERT-VANCE (60s, pearls, a black veil pushed back), the widow of the museum's founder, perched on a gilt chair.

DR. SIMEON ACHTERBERG (40s, bow tie, spectacles), the curator, nervously winding his own watch.

MISS CLARISSA BOONE (20s, secretary, red lipstick, a pencil behind her ear).

ELIAS THORNCROFT (30s, dark suit, an easy grin), the nephew and heir.

HOBBS (70s, white gloves, spine straight as a ruler), the butler.

                    FELL
          Thank you all for staying. It's a nasty night, and I'd hoped we'd be finished by supper.

                    ODETTE
          My husband is lying in the next room, Inspector. Supper is not a priority.

                    FELL
          Of course. Forgive me.

                    SIMEON
          The Regulator clock stopped at seven-forty. That's the time on the coroner's slip, isn't it?

                    FELL
          So it is.

                    CLARISSA
          Then he died at seven-forty. It's simple.

                    FELL
          It is, Miss Boone. Which is why I distrust it.

                    ELIAS
          Distrust a stopped clock? Inspector, you're a romantic.

                    FELL
          I distrust any clock that stops precisely when it's useful.

                    HOBBS
          If I may, sir.

                    FELL
          Please.

                    HOBBS
          The Regulator has not stopped on its own in thirty-one years. Mr. Lambert-Vance wound it every Sunday himself. He had a ritual. A key kept on a chain.

                    FELL
          And that key is now where?

                    HOBBS
          It was in his waistcoat pocket, sir.

                    FELL
          It was not. I checked.

A pendulum clicks. Nobody breathes.

                    ODETTE
          What are you suggesting?

                    FELL
          That someone wound the clock back to seven-forty so that someone else might have an alibi.

                    ELIAS
          I was in the library with Clarissa from seven-fifteen.

                    CLARISSA
          Yes. Yes, we were.

                    FELL
          You were. And Dr. Achterberg was in the cellar, and Hobbs was serving tea in the east wing, and Madame Lambert-Vance was dressing. Every person in this room has an alibi for seven-forty, which is why I believe the murder occurred at seven-ten.

                    SIMEON
          That's impossible. The tea service was at seven.

                    FELL
          And ended at seven-oh-five. Hobbs, how many cups did you collect?

                    HOBBS
          Five, sir.

                    FELL
          There are six people in this house.

A long pause. The chandelier trembles.

                    ODETTE
          (very quietly)
          My husband did not take tea after six.

                    FELL
          No. But someone took tea to him. And left the sixth cup upstairs, with lipstick on the rim.

All eyes turn to Clarissa. She touches her mouth. Smiles, without joy.
